<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Domesday Book</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1086">1086</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Domesday survey</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Domesday Book is the record of the great survey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> ordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> in 1085 and largely completed in 1086. It lists landholders, manors, resources and taxable values across most of the kingdom, giving an unmatched picture of the land after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a>. It also records local customs, including the Welsh laws of Archenfield on the border.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/domesday-book.jpg" alt="Domesday Book" width="320" height="173" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrews, William · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Domesday-book-1804x972.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
